From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valentin rameau <valentin.rameau@outlook.fr>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 06:43:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 29/33] PPU: count which register ports change between
 scanlines, and from where

A host that runs the frame's guest code first and renders all 224 lines
afterwards can only defer its per-line work if it knows what changes per line.
Measuring that from the HDMA engine alone is incomplete whenever the host also
dispatches a raster-split IRQ inside its render loop, because that is guest
code writing registers between scanlines through a path HDMA never touches.

g_ppu_line_write_src lets the host name the source that is currently running
(per-line HDMA, the raster-split IRQ, its own per-line code) and ppu_write
charges each register write to it, per port. Zero outside the draw loop, which
includes the whole vblank CPU half, so the counts are mid-frame only. One
predictable branch per register write when nothing armed it.

On F-Zero it found exactly the gap it was written for: the raster-split IRQ
writes $2105 (BGMODE) and $2131 (CGADSUB) mid-frame, neither of which appears
in the HDMA stream, and a replay without $2105 would draw the whole frame in
one BG mode -- which is precisely what a raster split exists to avoid.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 runner/src/snes/ppu.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 runner/src/snes/ppu.h |  6 ++++++
 2 files changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
index ad51cba..1cca622 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
@@ -3192,7 +3192,25 @@ void ppu_rasterApplyLine(Ppu *ppu, int line) {
   }
 }
 
+/* TEMPORARY PROBE (strategy C): which PPU ports actually change
+ * BETWEEN scanlines, and from where. Session 6 measured the data ports (VRAM,
+ * CGRAM, OAM: zero mid-frame writes, all in vblank) and the HDMA register
+ * stream, and concluded 16 bytes/line would be enough to journal and replay a
+ * deferred wide pass. It did not cover the fourth source: this host dispatches
+ * the raster-split IRQ INLINE in the draw loop (fzero_rtl.c calls
+ * bank_00_8601 at the trigger line), so guest code runs between scanlines and
+ * whatever it writes to $2100-$213F never passed through HDMA.
+ *
+ * The host sets g_ppu_line_write_src to say which source is running; every
+ * write is then charged to it, per port. Off (0) at every other moment, which
+ * includes the whole vblank CPU half, so the counts are mid-frame only.
+ * One predictable branch per register write when the probe is not armed. */
+int g_ppu_line_write_src;                    /* 0 = off / not in the draw loop */
+unsigned long g_ppu_line_writes[4][0x40];    /* [src][port & 0x3f] */
+
 void ppu_write(Ppu* ppu, uint8_t adr, uint8_t val) {
+  if (g_ppu_line_write_src)
+    g_ppu_line_writes[g_ppu_line_write_src & 3][adr & 0x3f]++;
 //  if (adr != 24 && adr != 25)
 //    printf("ppu_write(%d, %d)\n", adr, val);
   switch(adr) {
diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
index 459b880..2f4e337 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
@@ -677,6 +677,12 @@ void PpuSetExtraSpace(Ppu *ppu, uint16_t extra);
 // margin's columns in screen order first, then the right margin's.
 void PpuSetMode7MarginTiles(Ppu *ppu, const uint8_t *tiles);
 
+/* TEMPORARY PROBE, see ppu.c. A host that runs work between scanlines sets
+ * this to name the source (1 = per-line HDMA, 2 = raster-split IRQ, 3 = the
+ * host's own per-line code) and back to 0 outside the draw loop; every PPU
+ * register write is then counted per source and port. */
+extern int g_ppu_line_write_src;
+extern unsigned long g_ppu_line_writes[4][0x40];
 
